  "# ADR-2607183900: Kotoba component language and relative runtime roles

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-18
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Context

The initial qualification shorthand conflated `.kotoba` source, component
trust, and the runtime relationship between an importer and exporter. It would
unnecessarily keep HTTP, database, orchestration and policy logic in CLJC even
when those implementations can remain capability-safe.

## Decision

`.kotoba` denotes canonical capability-safe component source. It does not
denote guest-only or pure-only code. Every external effect remains explicit in
the source effect closure and emitted imports; there is no ambient eval,
require, host interop, syscall or self-minted grant.

Host and guest are relative runtime roles. A component is a guest of the
runtime or provider supplying its imports, and it is a host to a downstream
component whose imports it implements. A `.kotoba` provider may therefore
import lower-level capabilities and export a narrower higher-level
capability. HTTP and database components may be written in Kotoba, but writing
a provider in the same language never bypasses its declared imports.

Kototama is the component tender and linker. It admits emitted artifacts,
instantiates components, links declared imports and exports, binds grants and
enforces resource limits. Aiueos remains the grant-policy broker. The minimal
native TCB still owns Wasm engine control, raw syscall binding, root secret
custody and grant-verification roots.

Q9 migrations classify logic by imports, exports and TCB boundary. Domain,
orchestration, validation, protocol and provider logic may move to `.kotoba`;
CLJC may remain temporarily as oracle, compatibility adapter or native
primitive. Existing CI, soak, rollback and explicit consumer-cutover gates are
unchanged.

## Language safety maturity checkpoint

The restricted Web path now owns bounded parametric algebraic values rather
than delegating their semantics to JavaScript shape conventions.

- Kotoba-script PR 14 (`d57ec04f`) and compiler PR 73 (`ec4dcbf1`) introduced
  canonical `[:result ok-type err-type]` descriptors with depth-8/node-64
  type and runtime budgets.
- Kotoba-script PR 15 (`f9b342a8`) and compiler PR 74 (`a152314f`) introduced
  exhaustive `match-result`: one typed ok branch and one typed err branch are
  mandatory and only the selected branch executes.
- Kotoba-script PR 16 (`8366a2db`) and compiler PR 76 (`9d36d887`) introduced
  user-defined closed variants:
  `[:variant :qualified/type [[:case payload-type] ...]]`. Variants admit at
  most 32 unique cases inside the same depth/node budget. Every runtime value
  carries the complete descriptor, case tag and payload, so a same-named case
  from another schema cannot substitute across a boundary. `match-variant`
  requires every declared case exactly once in declaration order and admits no
  wildcard that could silently absorb schema expansion.
- Kotoba-script PR 17 (`3508a7e7dbff4a7257ce2ac356eb118e3afd2113`)
  and compiler PR 77 (`2d53a28f5e401a6240d2b1df48455cd697acd29b`)
  completed reference and restricted-JavaScript semantics for generic
  `[:option payload-type]`. `none` is represented as `[descriptor false]` and
  therefore retains its complete type identity instead of using JavaScript
  null/undefined or an untyped sentinel; `some` is
  `[descriptor true payload]`. Exact descriptor equality, payload validation,
  lazy fallback and exhaustive `match-option` are covered in kotoba-script by
  19 tests / 74 assertions and in the compiler target corpus by 46 tests / 391
  assertions.
- Kotoba-script PR 18 (`cfc9edcd6cd8c7662ae327972939f3060479e195`)
  and compiler PR 78 (`19c4e02f0b531b811a0d3071aa60b25f2fbaa8de`)
  introduced fixed heterogeneous vectors as
  `[:vector [item-type ...]]`. At most 32 statically typed positions fit
  inside the shared descriptor budget. Canonical values carry the complete
  descriptor followed by the exact item sequence; construction, literal-index
  projection, persistent replacement and structural equality revalidate the
  descriptor, length and every position. Dynamic indexes, sparse values,
  append/drop and host mutation are not admitted.
- Kotoba-script PR 19 (`57046fa5ce5a7fa05bba6b967cf2389e613e4336`)
  and compiler PR 79 (`db45bb82d8df9893b662bf10071db579f26d7f84`)
  introduced bounded typed sets as `[:set item-type]`, with at most 32
  recursively validated values. Their canonical ABI is
  `[descriptor, sorted-items]`; sorting uses a Kotoba-owned total order over
  every admitted value family, and duplicate boundary or constructor inputs
  fail closed instead of inheriting JavaScript collection behavior. Count,
  membership, persistent insertion/removal and structural equality preserve
  exact descriptor identity. Nested sets and algebraic payloads are covered.
- Kotoba-script PR 20 (`b2437c76c5ba44e0644203141cd87ab50d23aaf3`)
  and compiler PR 80 (`0dcdb34bd4a28328106ec8c0ab35ca94dfbe3b84`)
  introduced nominal bounded records as
  `[:record :qualified/type [[:field field-type] ...]]`. Canonical values are
  `[descriptor, field-value ...]`, with 1--32 unique keyword fields in
  declaration order under the shared descriptor budget. Construction requires
  exact arity; projection and persistent replacement require a declared
  keyword literal; every boundary validates nominal schema identity, arity and
  field values recursively. Cross-schema substitution, dynamic/unknown fields,
  sparse host objects, prototype behavior, mutation and object identity remain
  outside the language ABI.
- Kotoba-script PR 21 (`dbda10bf9606f9ac6a09cf38005831128bf6e366`)
  and compiler PR 81 (`c802d234f81de7c468e227512d7874c6e7c7b280`)
  sealed floating point as the versioned fail-closed policy
  `:kotoba.floating-point/forbidden-v1` and the Web artifact field
  `floatingPointPolicy: 'forbidden-v1'`. Every compilation result carries the
  policy. Floating literals, descriptors, parameters, results, operations,
  NaN, infinity and boundary values are rejected before lowering; JavaScript
  numbers, signed zero, implicit rounding and integer-to-double conversion
  cannot silently become Kotoba semantics. Any future floating profile requires
  a new ABI version and explicit cross-backend conformance evidence.
- Compiler PR 82 (`c7358bbeb0410dd082ab16d49dff8dffcc763a82`)
  established one versioned machine-readable typed-value conformance corpus.
  The same six positive vectors and eight negative vectors now qualify generic
  options, parametric results, closed variants, heterogeneous vectors, typed
  sets, nominal records and floating-point denial across the reference
  interpreter and restricted Web emitter. `.cljk` is confirmed as a Kotoba
  source extension selecting this compiler, not a JVM runtime or an independent
  typed-value ABI.
- Compiler PR 83 (`0246c205eb2f7dba2a3be303b16ca653f3a9f83f`)
  sealed `kotoba.typed/v1` into the Wasm artifact and made the browser host
  parse and validate it before instantiation. Forged and cross-schema
  externrefs fail closed at exported parameter and result boundaries.
- Compiler PR 84 (`bab9d7f1f73a629584652ccf585b2b96e320b980`)
  qualified the first `kotoba.typed/externref-v1` Wasm subset for generic
  option/result matching, closed variants, heterogeneous vectors, typed sets
  and nominal records. The shared positive corpus now executes on reference,
  restricted Web and Wasm; malformed duplicate sets and boundary substitution
  remain rejected. Unsupported typed Wasm operations fail during lowering.
- Compiler PR 85 (`36c0fd2a4dfa606f670d69e7634881fa8361b792`)
  adds predicates, lazy option/result fallback extraction, heterogeneous-vector
  and record update/equality, and typed-set contains/conj/disj/equality. Its
  guarded gate passed 250 tests / 3,649 assertions, including real Node
  WebAssembly execution. All non-Windows CI passed; Windows stopped at the
  pre-existing fail-closed WFP admission probe before feature tests.
- Compiler PR 86 (`bb633435309cf4781b82e5e48c1225bc02570a20`)
  defines one closed discovery
  contract for `.kotoba`, `.cljk`, and `.cljc`. JVM tooling and the JVM-free nbb
  path share it; extensions do not select a runtime. Explicit JS and Wasm target
  tests passed locally (252 tests / 3,669 assertions), and real nbb probes
  compiled `.cljk` to a valid Wasm module and admitted `.cljc` without JVM use.
  All non-Windows CI passed twice; Windows remained fail-closed at the known
  WFP admission probe before feature tests. This closes G2.
- Compiler PR 87 (`2553fbda255c4711b60a33e030734b9c83f49568`)
  starts G3 with
  `kotoba.diagnostic/v1`: stable phase codes, error severity, redacted source
  basename and bounded source spans. JVM tools.reader and the JVM-free nbb
  reader preserve locations through constant substitution and desugaring.
  The guarded JVM gate passes 253 tests / 3,673 assertions; the nbb diagnostic
  probe reports the same `:kotoba/source-rejected` code at line 2, column 3.
  All non-Windows CI, including the full nbb corpus, passed twice. Structured
  diagnostics are complete for G3.
- Compiler PR 88 (`2603952bc6e5af9cf76fdd57aedb9a1541b6a3fe`) adds sealed
  `kotoba.interface/v1` inspection for `.kotoba`, `.cljk`, and `.cljc`.
  Only public names, arities, parameter/result types and effects are exposed;
  private functions, bodies, constants, source and local paths are excluded.
  Local guarded verification passed 255 tests / 3,681 assertions, and all
  non-Windows CI passed twice. Windows remained fail-closed at the known WFP
  admission probe. Interface inspection is complete for G3.
- Compiler PR 89 (`6d7b93277323920fb53cd10012f1572e562cd442`) adds
  `kotoba.compatibility/v1` to JS manifests, Wasm custom sections and sealed
  native artifacts. It binds compiler, language, KIR, selected target, runtime,
  value ABI and kototama tender contract identities. Browser/WASI hosts reject
  duplicate, malformed and mismatched metadata before instantiation, and the
  native verifier recomputes and checks the descriptor. Guarded verification
  passes 257 tests / 3,690 assertions, the 15-case nbb corpus, browser-host
  tests and syntax validation. The public browser/WASI runtime now requires
  metadata and rejects its absence; hand-authored test modules receive an
  explicit sealed fixture section rather than a production bypass.
- Kototama PR 38 (`423ab57d5813cc07a55e9a2a953dbe0f28b1e011`) parses the bounded compatibility
  section before Chicory instantiation and validates compiler, language, KIR,
  generic Wasm target, runtime, value ABI, tender role and tender contract.
  Sessions declaring a Kotoba compiler artifact reject missing metadata.
  Its guarded main-branch suite passes 105 tests / 327 assertions and both CI
  gates passed. Compiler PR 89 passed all repeated Linux, Linux arm64, Safari,
  Android, server-kind and one macOS run; one duplicate macOS run encountered a
  transient native compiler-tool query failure, and Windows remained at the
  known WFP admission probe. Compatibility metadata is complete for G3.
- Compiler PR 90 (`c77da1c6e842ecdee571f8b576a9b26b5e3e2229`) adds sealed
  `kotoba.provenance/v1`. It binds source, policy, build metadata, HIR, KIR,
  target profile, compatibility and every primary/binary/object output digest.
  JS manifests embed it and every CLI target writes a provenance sidecar.
  Guarded verification passes 258 tests / 3,725 assertions. All repeated
  non-Windows CI passed; Windows stayed at the known WFP probe. Artifact
  provenance is complete for G3.
- Compiler PR 91 (`b2ecdbdaa1bab85ca639577d8a63204b7877fb1a`) adds signed
  `kotoba.incremental-cache/v1` admission. Cache hits require exact current
  input, target, provenance and payload identities plus trusted non-revoked
  signer and validity window. Rejected hits never silently fall back to a fresh
  compile. Guarded verification passes 260 tests / 3,738 assertions. All
  repeated non-Windows CI passed and Windows stayed at the known WFP probe.
  Safe incremental-cache admission is complete for G3.
- Installed-release evidence
  `90-docs/evidence/260719-kotoba-v0.6.1-installed-release.edn` records the
  published Darwin arm64 asset and checksum. The isolated shell install and the
  Homebrew Cellar binary both run the same `.kotoba`, `.cljk`, and `.cljc`
  corpus to 42; the shell binary rejects `eval`. This does not close the gate:
  a pre-existing non-Homebrew `/opt/homebrew/bin/kotoba` prevents formula
  linking and `brew test`, the negative path emits an unstructured JVM stack
  trace, and v0.6.1 predates compiler PR 90/91.
- Kotoba PR 360 (`985fea6c13ee127db2651e6092a7e8a1f1dce50f`) closes the observed negative-path
  disclosure for the next release. The process boundary converts `run/failed`
  into `kotoba.diagnostic/v1`, retains only the source basename and bounded
  runtime problems, and excludes absolute paths, exception messages and Java
  stack frames. Its guarded suite passes 345 tests / 1,614 assertions and both
  CLJ launcher and Python SDK CI gates passed.
- Kotoba PR 361 (`1c025d81e417407a1968f7108bbb89a3f8adafb0`) pins compiler PR 91 and makes native publication
  fail closed on the full source test suite plus a JVM-free installed-binary
  corpus. The corpus executes identical `.kotoba`, `.cljk`, and `.cljc`
  programs to 42, requires a path-free namespaced `kotoba.diagnostic/v1` for a
  forbidden `eval`, rejects script/JVM launchers by native executable magic,
  binds the evidence packet to the binary SHA-256, and publishes one packet per
  platform. It also preserves namespaced diagnostic values on the JSON wire
  and makes successful test runs terminate deterministically. Local guarded
  verification passes 345 tests / 1,616 assertions. The non-publishing native
  matrix passed Darwin arm64, Darwin amd64 and Linux amd64, including the
  JVM-free verifier and evidence packaging. The release gate is merged.
- Release v0.6.22 publishes all three native archives, checksums and
  per-platform evidence packets from that merged gate. The isolated shell
  installer verifies the Darwin arm64 checksum and passes the `.kotoba`,
  `.cljk`, `.cljc` = 42 corpus plus path-free structured `eval` rejection.
  Kotoba PR 362 and homebrew-kotoba PR 25 update both formula copies to the
  exact v0.6.22 resource checksums without a Clojure/JVM dependency. Homebrew
  downloads and verifies the resource, but this host refuses installation
  before staging because Apple Command Line Tools 26.3 is not installed.
  Existing `/opt/homebrew/bin/kotoba` was not overwritten. Evidence is archived
  in `90-docs/evidence/260719-kotoba-v0.6.22-installed-release.edn`.
- Homebrew bottle workflow run 29655656220 installs and executes
  `brew test --verbose` successfully on macOS arm64, macOS Intel and Linux
  amd64. Homebrew-kotoba PR 26 seals the three immutable bottle digests into
  the Formula. The local arm64 bottle installs into Cellar and passes the same
  native three-extension and structured-rejection corpus. Linking remains
  intentionally skipped because the unrelated pre-existing command is outside
  this migration's authority; installed behavior is verified by exact Cellar
  path. This closes G3.
- Compiler PR 92 (`f529e7f594cc996d7a74dc7994c5f536ac6e780e`) closes two
  migration defects without opening dynamic evaluation: a one-string `def`
  is now a value rather than an implicit docstring; `(keyword "literal")` is
  folded only for a closed literal; and `cond->` lowers to ordered,
  single-evaluation `let`/`if` persistent updates. Dynamic keyword inputs and
  non-call updates remain rejected. Guarded verification passes 263 tests /
  3,745 assertions; all non-Windows CI passes, while Windows remains at the
  known fail-closed WFP probe.
- Etzhayyim tashikame PR 8
  (`7f6ec41e83aee61315be9f57870ff81d954009c3`) is the first completed
  installed-release organization pilot. Its closed two-module `.kotoba`
  project is checked, compiled and executed with v0.6.22, carries project and
  provenance digests, rejects forbidden `eval`, retains the CLJC rollback
  implementation, and now matches the original unknown-phase fallback instead
  of silently denying it.
- The five-organization pilot set is now implemented with the installed
  v0.6.22 release. Kotoba-lang PR 26 (`aa1fc7faa170d9d3813b4c06087b074ecb2d6524`)
  executes the namespace-priority utility kernel to 42; cloud-itonami PR 3
  (`60f2e0323feab5cf906e4bd854ddfbb8c35db803`) checks all 32 governor flag
  combinations; gftdcojp cloud-mamori PR 1
  (`6df62179dadb8db3304f2ac395b0b9a56205ee30`) checks a closed typed-keyword
  referral boundary; and com-junkawasaki ghosthacker-lightning PR 1
  (`87571cf46d722482933e86b3a2e14ca5d4ad38f1`) checks signed timing boundary
  values. Every pilot has local installed-release compile, Node execution,
  empty-capability/provenance checks and forbidden-`eval` rejection. The first
  three organizations have hosted CI evidence. Cloud-mamori did not schedule
  its new workflow, and ghosthacker-lightning's two jobs failed before any
  step ran, so those two hosted evidence paths remain operational gaps rather
  than language-test failures.
- The com-junkawasaki pilot also exposed a defense-in-depth false positive:
  the generated safe identifier derived from `window-ms` matched the broad
  `window` output regex before AST verification. Renaming it to `threshold-ms`
  produced an otherwise identical accepted artifact. Kotoba-script PR 22
  (`4c9a594e5bfd1b794a42acc4d7c92c9212019ccf`) makes the parsed AST
  authoritative for ambient globals, dynamic imports, property access and
  object-literal forbidden keys. Safe identifiers, strings and comments no
  longer collide with authority names, while escaped `globalThis`,
  `constructor`, `__proto__` and actual ambient globals remain rejected. Its
  guarded suite passes 24 tests / 103 assertions.
- Compiler PR 93 (`12810ac6615bef9b54e1f11da4d452fe15c31012`)
  pins that backend and compiles/executes the real `window-ms` timing boundary
  to `1,0,0`. Guarded verification passes 264 tests / 3,748 assertions; every
  non-Windows push and pull-request job passed, and Windows stopped at the
  unchanged fail-closed WFP network-denial probe. Kotoba PR 363
  (`e3cbae8e8b0f607a11fd19e5a75e37652e7f9eb1`) pins compiler PR 93 and adds
  the same Web compile/runtime case to the JVM-free native release evidence.
  Its guarded suite passes 346 tests / 1,618 assertions and both CI gates pass.
  Release v0.6.23 passes the Darwin arm64, Darwin amd64 and Linux amd64 native
  matrix in workflow run 29657545712. Each job passed the source suite,
  native-image build, JVM-free verifier, syntax-aware authority-name runtime
  probe, evidence recording and immutable asset publication. The downloaded
  Darwin arm64 archive checksum and evidence packet were independently
  verified, and that installed binary compiled/executed the timing pilot.
  Homebrew-kotoba PR 27 (`bc8696155916b4d8fe97ae3905722becb4003649`)
  updates all source/resource checksums and adds the same safe-name formula
  test. Bottle workflow run 29657900081 passes real install/test on macOS
  arm64, macOS Intel and Linux amd64. Homebrew-kotoba PR 28
  (`bed3f06ad9bbf355464bdb94a36d117ecebc3d72`) seals all three bottle
  digests, and kotoba PR 364
  (`1f1c2b7b1024f994cf8dabe0c662548feaa5cba0`) synchronizes the in-repository
  Formula. The local arm64 bottle installs at the exact Cellar path and passes
  safe-name compile/runtime plus structured rejection. The unrelated existing
  `/opt/homebrew/bin/kotoba` remains untouched. Installed v0.6.23 supply-chain
  evidence is complete.

The latest merged compiler gate passed 250 tests / 3,664 assertions locally for
PR 83 and 250 tests / 3,649 assertions for PR 84's then-current suite. Linux,
Linux arm64, macOS including iOS AOT/simulator, Android NDK, server-kind,
Safari and Ubuntu browser checks passed in both push and pull-request events.
Windows jobs encountered the pre-existing hosted-runner WFP probe failure
(timeout or WSAECONNREFUSED instead of required WSAEACCES) before feature tests;
the network-denial predicate was not weakened. Compiler-to-restricted-ESM-to-
Node behavior and reference/Web positive and negative boundary corpora passed.

This completes G1 and closes canonical finite variant, generic option, fixed heterogeneous
vector, bounded typed-set and nominal record identity, bounded typed payloads,
and generalized exhaustive matching on reference/Web. It does not complete the
language or fleet migration.

## Remaining work and completion gap

Completion is divided into four ordered gates. Passing a later gate cannot
compensate for an earlier safety gap.

### G1: safe language semantics -- complete

Generic option, fixed heterogeneous vectors, bounded typed sets and nominal
bounded records are complete for the qualified reference/Web subset. Floating
point is explicitly excluded by `forbidden-v1`; NaN, infinity, signed zero,
rounding and conversion cannot enter through an unversioned host fallback.

G1 is complete only when each admitted value family has one canonical typed
value ABI, bounded validation, no JavaScript sentinel shortcut, lazy branch
semantics where applicable, and matching positive/negative behavior in the
reference interpreter and restricted Web emitter.

### G2: backend parity -- complete

The artifact-sealed Wasm ABI, strict boundary admission, shared corpus,
algebraic operations and explicit `.cljk`/`.cljc` source-discovery and
selected-target coverage now exist. `.cljk` has no separate runtime ABI: selecting Web must
use the qualified restricted-JavaScript ABI and selecting Wasm must use the
qualified Wasm ABI. JVM may execute build tooling but is never a Kotoba runtime
target. Backend-specific coercion, host object identity, integer handles without
owned lifetime semantics, or permissive fallback is a qualification failure.

### G3: toolchain and supply-chain qualification -- complete

Add stable structured diagnostics, source locations, interface inspection,
compiler/runtime/kototama compatibility metadata, artifact provenance and
safe incremental-cache admission. Produce reproducible release artifacts and
prove both `brew install` and the shell installer by compiling and running the
same installed-release corpus; tests against repository-local binaries do not
count as release evidence. Compiler provenance, signed cache admission and the
native release gate are implemented. Version v0.6.26, source/native archive
checksums, clean-prefix shell evidence and the three-platform Homebrew bottle
